WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.100
CORS and CSRF prevention solve different problems.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.680
CORS controls which browser JavaScript origins may read certain cross-origin responses.

3
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:15.580
CSRF prevention helps validate that a state-changing browser request is intended by the authenticated user.

4
00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:21.360
Confusing those controls can lead to policies that look security-related but answer the wrong question.

5
00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:23.880
Authentication identifies the caller.

6
00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:27.500
Authorization decides whether the caller is allowed to act.

7
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:31.520
CORS describes browser cross-origin read permission.

8
00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:37.220
Antiforgery validation helps confirm request intent for protected state-changing browser requests.

9
00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.480
Each control has a job, and a secure design keeps those jobs separate.

10
00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.960
Origin and Referer validation can be useful defense-in-depth signals in some designs.

11
00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:53.300
They can help identify unexpected browser request contexts or support additional review of sensitive workflows.

12
00:00:53.400 --> 00:01:04.680
But in a normal ASP.NET Core design, they should not become the only CSRF control for protected unsafe requests when framework antiforgery validation is appropriate.

13
00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.680
Boundary confusion often appears during troubleshooting.

14
00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:18.260
A browser may show a CORS error, an API may return an authentication failure, an authorization policy may deny access, or an antiforgery check may reject a request.

15
00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.080
Those outcomes point to different controls.

16
00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:26.220
Teams should diagnose which control is speaking before changing configuration.

17
00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.660
Safe review questions help.

18
00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:31.160
Does this endpoint change state?

19
00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.660
Does it use browser cookies?

20
00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:35.920
Does it need antiforgery validation?

21
00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:38.740
Which origins may read responses?

22
00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.960
Which users or services are authenticated?

23
00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.140
Which authorization policy applies?

24
00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.720
Which cookie SameSite settings are intended?

25
00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:53.780
Clear answers prevent CORS, authentication, authorization, and CSRF controls from being mixed together.
